1. Readiness Probe (Can I receive traffic?)

Purpose: Determines whether a pod is ready to serve requests.

If the readiness probe fails:

Pod is NOT restarted
Pod is removed from the Service endpoints
Kubernetes stops sending traffic to it
Example Scenario

Your application takes 30 seconds to load configuration and connect to a database.

YAML
readinessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /ready
port: 8080
initialDelaySeconds: 5
periodSeconds: 10
Flow
Plain Text
Pod Starts
|
v
Application Initializing
|
Readiness = Fail
|
Service does NOT send traffic
|
Application Ready
|
Readiness = Pass
|
Traffic Starts
Common Use Cases
Waiting for database connection
Cache warm-up
Loading configuration
Waiting for dependent services
2. Liveness Probe (Am I alive?)

Purpose: Determines whether the application is still functioning.

If the liveness probe fails:

Kubernetes assumes the application is hung/dead
Pod is restarted automatically
Example Scenario

Java application entered a deadlock.

YAML
livenessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /health
port: 8080
initialDelaySeconds: 30
periodSeconds: 10
Flow
Plain Text
Pod Running
|
Application Hangs
|
Liveness = Fail
|
Kubernetes Restarts Pod
Common Use Cases
Deadlock detection
Application stuck in infinite loop
Memory corruption
Hung process
Both Together
YAML
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: demo
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: myapp:latest
 
readinessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /ready
port: 8080
initialDelaySeconds: 5
periodSeconds: 5
 
livenessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /health
port: 8080
initialDelaySeconds: 30
periodSeconds: 10
Timeline
Plain Text
Time 0 Pod Starts
Time 5 Readiness checks start
Time 20 App Ready
Service sends traffic
 
Time 30 Liveness checks start
 
App hangs at Time 100
Liveness fails
Kubernetes restarts pod
Startup Probe (Third Probe Often Asked in Interviews)

For slow-starting applications:

YAML
startupProbe:
httpGet:
path: /health
port: 8080
failureThreshold: 30
periodSeconds: 10

While startup probe is running:

Readiness is ignored
Liveness is ignored

Useful for:

Java Spring Boot
Large .NET applications
Applications taking several minutes to start
Interview Question

Q: What happens if Readiness Probe fails?

✅ Pod continues running
 ✅ No restart happens
 ✅ Pod removed from Service endpoints

Q: What happens if Liveness Probe fails?

✅ Pod restarted
 ✅ New container instance starts

Quick Memory Trick
Plain Text
Readiness = Ready to receive traffic?
 
Liveness = Alive or dead?
 
Startup = Finished starting?

For a mid-to-senior Kubernetes interview, remember this key statement:

Readiness controls traffic flow; Liveness controls container restart; Startup protects slow-starting applications from premature restarts.

ResourceQuota limits aggregate resource usage for a namespace.
LimitRange sets default/min/max resource constraints for individual pods or containers.
Both are commonly used together in production clusters.
